iqr — Interquartile range                                              data · tier 1 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    data.iqr(var, type: Int = 7)

DESCRIPTION
    The distance between the third and the first quartile, `quantile(var, 0.75) - quantile(var, 0.25)`,
    under the quantile definition `type:` names (the nine definitions of Hyndman and Fan, as `quantile`
    offers them; 7 unless given, R's default). A robust measure of spread: half of the values lie within it,
    and one outlier cannot move it. The quartiles are taken over the values present, with the reducers'
    warning for the rows with `NA` skipped (section 4.1 of the Language Reference), once for each quartile;
    one value gives 0. Like R's `IQR(x, type =)`.

ARGUMENTS
    data        Frame. The frame.
    var         Var. The variable.
    type:       Int = 7. The quantile definition, 1 to 9; 7 unless given.

VALUE
    The range, a Float, printed as `mean` prints.

DETAILS
    The two quartiles come from `quantile` under one definition and are subtracted; nothing else is
    computed, so the result agrees with `quantile` to the bit.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    W0201   rows with NA in the variable, or in the weights, were skipped
    W0205   the quantile falls between Inf and -Inf
    Note    rows with zero weight were left out
    Z0101   the variable, a `by:` variable or the weights variable is not in the frame
    Z0401   p: is not a probability, type: is not 1 to 9, the variable is not numeric, or the weights are
            unusable
    Z0406   p: is missing

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Hyndman, R. J. and Fan, Y. (1996) Sample quantiles in statistical packages, The American Statistician 50(4), 361–365.
    R Core Team, the `IQR` function of the stats package.

SEE ALSO
    quantile, mad, sd, median

EXAMPLES
    > let d = frame(x = [1, 2, 4, 7, 11])
    > d.iqr(x)
    5
    > d.iqr(x, type: 6)
    7.5
